---
/**
 * The changelog (docs/decisions.md D29, D31).
 *
 * Freshness is structural, not promised: a dated list of what actually changed, written
 * by hand, with the things that are still missing named on the same page. A site that
 * quietly stops being updated is worse than one that says what it has not done yet.
 *
 * Newest first. Entries are appended and facts are never quietly corrected — a correction
 * gets its own dated entry, the same rule as the decisions log. See src/lib/updates.ts for
 * what that rule does and does not cover.
 */
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import ChapterFurniture from "../components/ChapterFurniture.astro";
import { SITE_HOST } from "../lib/site";

import { ENTRIES, MISSING, fmtDate as fmt } from "../lib/updates";
---

<Page
  title="Updates"
  description={`A dated changelog for ${SITE_HOST} — what changed, when, and what is still missing.`}
>
  {/* The eyebrow used to repeat the title word for word, so the first two lines of the
      page were the same word twice. Elsewhere on the site the eyebrow carries a second
      piece of information — "Every build in the book" over "The 19 Builds". */}
  <ChapterFurniture
    eyebrow="What changed, and when"
    title="Updates"
    subtitle="Newest first."
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      {/* This used to say the printed book does not change between print runs, and then
          tell you what to do when a page number does not match — which cannot both be
          true. What is fixed is the copy in your hands; what moves is the numbering
          between one print run and the next. */}
      <p class="lede">
        This is the list of what changed on this site, and when. The book in your hands
        does not change — once it is printed, it is printed. But page numbers can shift
        between print runs, so if a number here does not match your copy,{" "}
        <a href="/editions">editions</a> tells you which run you are holding.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="log">
    <h2 id="log">What changed</h2>

    {
      ENTRIES.map((e) => (
        <article class="entry">
          <p class="entry__date">
            <time datetime={e.date}>{fmt(e.date)}</time>
          </p>
          <h3 class="entry__title">{e.title}</h3>
          <ul class="entry__items">
            {e.items.map((i) => (
              <li>{i}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="missing">
    <h2 id="missing">What is missing right now</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Named here rather than left for you to discover. Each one gets its own dated entry
        above on the day it arrives.
      </p>
      <ul class="plain">{MISSING.map((m) => <li>{m}</li>)}</ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="how">
    <h2 id="how">How this page works</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every entry is written by hand and added at the top. Nothing here gets quietly
        edited later — if something turns out to be wrong, the correction gets its own
        dated entry saying what was wrong.
      </p>
      {/* The address is the link text. It used to read "the feed is here. Paste that
          address into any feed reader" — with no address printed anywhere for "that" to
          point at, and on a phone no way to get one except long-press and Copy link.

          The certainty claim went too. "We have no way of knowing you subscribed" cannot
          sit two sections below "what the host's access logs contain" being named as an
          open question: a feed reader fetches the file from that same host. */}
      <p>
        You can follow this page without giving anyone anything. The feed is at{" "}
        <a href="/updates.xml">{SITE_HOST}/updates.xml</a> — copy that address into any
        feed reader. There is no newsletter and there will not be one: a newsletter needs
        an email address from a thirteen-year-old, which is the one thing this site exists
        not to ask for. A feed needs no account, no address and no permission. Your feed
        reader still asks our host for the file, the same as any page you open here, and
        what that host writes down is one of the things named on the missing list above.
        Nothing this site controls records that you subscribed.
      </p>
      {/* The pace note. A review asked for this and it is purely protective of the person at
          the centre of the project: a thirteen-year-old with a book out will be asked for
          more, faster, by people who mean well. Saying the pace out loud, in public, on the
          page that records every change, makes it a stated policy rather than something he
          has to defend one email at a time. */}
      <p>
        There is no schedule here. Ryan is thirteen, he is at school, and he races. Things
        get added when they are ready and not before, which sometimes means nothing changes
        for weeks. That is the pace, not a queue of things running late — and the gap
        between entries is not a sign that something is wrong.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-inline-size: 72rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: clamp(1.25rem, 5vw, 3rem);
  }

  .prose {
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
  }

  .prose p + p,
  .prose ul {
    margin-block-start: 0.9em;
  }

  .lede {
    margin-block-start: 1.75rem;
    font-size: var(--text-subtitle);
    line-height: var(--text-subtitle--line-height);
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  .section {
    margin-block-start: 3rem;
  }

  .section > h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-h2);
    line-height: var(--text-h2--line-height);
    color: var(--text-section);
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
  }

  .section > .prose {
    margin-block-start: 0.75rem;
  }

  .entry {
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
    margin-block-start: 1.5rem;
    padding-block-start: 1.25rem;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--rule-hairline);
  }

  .entry__date {
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: var(--text-label--letter-spacing);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    /* A changelog is a column of dates; they line up. */
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums slashed-zero;
  }

  .entry__title {
    margin-block-start: 0.35rem;
    font-size: var(--text-subtitle);
    line-height: var(--text-subtitle--line-height);
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  .entry__items,
  .plain {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .entry__items {
    margin-block-start: 0.75rem;
  }

  .entry__items li,
  .plain li {
    padding-block: 0.55rem;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule-hairline);
  }

  a {
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  a:hover {
    text-decoration-color: var(--accent-spine);
  }
</style>
